# docs/build/tools/record_policy/history.policy — the repo policy of the build-memory history guard
# (docs/build/tools/memory_guard.py; the skill's check-history.sh reads the same file when run without
# the repo hook). Written by SEED-02a (Round-11 Stage B, T2) at 2026-10-01T08:04:03Z (date -u).
#
# One rule per line; "#" starts a comment at the start of a line or after whitespace. Directives:
#   append-only <path-glob>                       the whole file only appends at EOF (no line removed or changed)
#   date <path-glob> <ERE>                        an added line matching ERE carries a record date (its first ISO
#                                                 date/time), judged by G1 (R1 not after its commit; R2 for acts)
#   act-when <path-glob> <ERE>                    (memory_guard.py only) dates found by "date" rules on this path are
#                                                 acts (R2 applies) only when the same change adds or removes a line
#                                                 matching ERE in the same TOML record; otherwise events (R1 only)
#   allow <path-glob> <expires ISO> <fixed text>  a future record date on a line containing the text is allowed
#                                                 until "expires" — and, in memory_guard.py, only for dates up to
#                                                 "expires"; an expired entry no longer exempts (R5)
#   exempt <path> <heading text>                  a generated ##/### region (heading contains the text) is not judged
#   archive <dir>                                 an extra living-archived destination for the LEDGER head
#   placeholder <token>                           a declared placeholder a fillable line may replace with real
#                                                 text (`______` is the blank convention: any ≥3-underscore run)
#   seed-commit <sha-prefix>                      (memory_guard.py only) a Stage-B seed commit: its
#                                                 append-only findings are exempt — the seed wrote
#                                                 corrections under seed_verify.py before the completed
#                                                 modes existed (B4 §6.1)
# An allow entry names its reason and the record that authorises it. Changing this file is reviewed like code.

# ── Archive (B3 §3.5; SEED-10) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Rounds 1–10 LEDGER head is archived byte-for-byte under memory-repair/ with a sha256 pointer.
archive docs/build/reports/memory-repair

# ── Declared placeholder tokens (B2 §7 `placeholder-fill`; P34.7) ────────────────────────────
# A line in a fillable position (a PHASE LOG bullet's PR field, a BUILD_INDEX row's PR/branch
# cell, a readout's `Disposition:`/`Signed by:` line, a closed run ledger's lines, a .jsonl
# record's stamped fields, a `Gate status:` line in a ticket's first commit) may change only by
# replacing one of these tokens with real text — every other byte is identical. A token written
# `re:<ERE>` names a token family: `DRAFT-*` covers the Stage-B draft requirement ids
# (`DRAFT-MEM-1`, `DRAFT-ENG-4`, …) that SEED-12's id map stamped with their final SIG-* ids —
# a fill, not a rewrite. `(filled at closeout)` is the run-ledger template's close-time marker.
placeholder PR pending
placeholder #TBD
placeholder <pending>
placeholder pending push
placeholder PENDING-COMMIT-SHA
placeholder (pending)
placeholder (filled at closeout)
placeholder ______
placeholder re:DRAFT-[A-Z]+-[0-9]+
placeholder pending at write

# ── Generated region (B3 §3.13; SEED-16, later P34.29's generator) ────────────────────────────────────────
# The heading is "### RETURN PASS — current"; it is written here without the leading "###" because
# check-history.sh strips " #…" as a comment, which would drop the heading text.
exempt docs/build/LEDGER.md RETURN PASS — current

# ── db/sqitch.plan (B1 NEW-6; B4 G2 amendment 3; ADR-146; C-10) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The plan only appends at EOF: a planned_at is part of a sqitch change id, so a line is never edited or
# re-stamped. A new line's planned_at is a record date (G1 R1: not later than its commit).
append-only db/sqitch.plan
date db/sqitch.plan [0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}
# C-10 (the operator, GATE-P round 21, 2026-10-01T04:59:05Z; inspection 04:59:41Z): plan lines L44–52 keep
# their recorded, future-dated planned_at values — a local database holds them as stamped, so they are NEVER
# re-stamped; their true dates are corrected by appended record only (ADR-146, the correction ADR). They are
# allow-listed by change (name, dependencies and planned_at — the inputs of the change id) until the latest of
# them, 2026-10-19T21:00:00Z, after which every one of them is in the past.
