# Stage-B seeds and the P34.18/ADR-178 re-key. Its subject named ticket ids (`P35.14a/b`,
# `P36.3`, …), so `executed()` (B2 §7) marked the seven contracts "executing" mid-range and every
# rewritten skeleton line read as a frozen-contract edit (658 append-only findings, all attributed
# to 427c5efb — verified by the docs job's history run, run 37363207636). The rewrite is the
# sanctioned deliverable, not a correction of an executed contract; the branch rule forbids
# force-push, so the exempting declaration lands as a later commit rather than a reworded subject.
# Consequence recorded for later contexts: these seven files now read "executed" in any range that
# contains 427c5efb, and in later ranges they read executed once runs/<id>.md or a BUILD_INDEX row
# exists at base — i.e., normal BM-TICKET-04 semantics resume; subsequent edits must conform to it.
# Scope: findings attributed to 427c5efb only; no other commit borrows the exemption.
seed-commit 427c5efb

# ── P34.22a whole-tree future-date test — first-run entries ──────────────────────────────────────
# tests/unit/test_no_future_date_literals.py (P34.22a) sweeps code, data, migrations and tests for
# ISO literals later than HEAD. Real-world and scheduled values in comment-capable files carry
# `future-ok:` markers instead; the entries below cover the literals that cannot carry an inline
# marker — JSON fixtures (no comments) and values embedded inside multi-line strings — each by its
# own fixed text, each with an expiry just past the covered literal so the entry dies with the
# value it names (R5: an expired entry no longer exempts).
